\section{One bit, audited: what label-free evidence can and cannot supply}\label{sec:onebit}

The budget quantities of the preceding sections ($\beta$ on the calibration drift
$\gamma$, the transfer level $\eta$, the drift radius $B$, the majority-above-chance
anchor) share an uncomfortable epistemic status: each is user-supplied and none is
verifiable from unlabeled data---the same status as the calibration ``crutches'' this
paper criticizes. This section resolves that tension, in both directions, with three
results. First, the \emph{one-bit theorem}: within a corrected and falsifiable structural
class, label-free evidence identifies \emph{everything about the decision problem except
a single global sign bit}---and every identifying assumption in this literature is
exactly a selector of that bit. Second, the \emph{budget audit}: budgets can never be
verified label-free, but they can be \emph{falsified} at level $\alpha$, because the
drift $\gamma$ becomes point-estimable up to the bit; the blind set of the audit is
characterized exactly and is provably irreducible. Third, the \emph{evidence-channel
rate}: within the audited class the label-free channel attains the same $m^{-1/2}$ rate
as labeled paired benefits---labels buy a margin-dependent constant and the bit, not the
rate. Together these convert the critique ``the central guarantee is conditional on an
untestable premise'' into a precise accounting of \emph{which part} is untestable (one
bit, provably), \emph{which part} is testable (everything else, with explicit tests and
radii), and \emph{what it costs} (constants, not rates).

\paragraph{Positioning (deltas, not firsts).} The rank-one agreement algebra below is
classical: \textbf{Parisi, Strino, Nadler \& Kluger} (PNAS 2014) and \textbf{Jaffe,
Nadler \& Kluger} (AISTATS 2015) estimate classifier accuracies from agreement structure
under conditional independence, resolving the residual sign by \emph{assuming}
better-than-chance behavior; \textbf{Ibrahim, Fu \& Sidiropoulos} (NeurIPS 2019) prove
pairwise-co-occurrence identifiability up to label permutation; \textbf{Platanios et
al.} (UAI 2014; ICML 2016; NeurIPS 2017) estimate accuracies from agreement rates;
\textbf{Steinhardt \& Liang} (NeurIPS 2016) estimate risk under conditional-independence
structure; \textbf{Jaffe et al.} (AISTATS 2016) detect strong conditional-independence
violations; \textbf{Corrada-Emmanuel} (NeurIPS 2024; the NTQR program) develops
purely algebraic label-free evaluation with logical-consistency alarms; \textbf{Vovk et
al.} test exchangeability online by martingale (falsify-not-verify); \textbf{Rosenfeld
\& Garg} (NeurIPS 2023) bound target error under an explicit, unverifiable assumption.
Our contributions relative to this body are: (i) the \emph{exact insufficiency} of plain
conditional error-independence for the rank-one law, with the minimal fix
(Theorem~\ref{thm:H-deficit}); (ii) the \emph{information-theoretic necessity} statement
that the residual indeterminacy is exactly one bit at \emph{every} moment order, and that
each published identifying device is a bit-selector (Theorems~\ref{thm:onebit-id}--\ref{thm:bit-dictionary});
(iii) a \emph{statistical} level-$\alpha$ budget-falsification test with a proven
finite-sample radius, its exact blind set, and a constructive proof that the
overdetermination diagnostic is sound but not complete
(Theorems~\ref{thm:audit}--\ref{thm:no-verify}); and (iv) matching $m^{-1/2}$ bounds for
the evidence channel (Theorem~\ref{thm:ev-rate}). None of (i)--(iv) appears in the works
above; conversely, we claim none of their estimators or algebra as new.

\subsection{The corrected structural hypothesis}\label{sec:H}

Condition throughout on the observable region $D$ and write $\pi:=P(Y{=}1\mid D)$. For a
panel $f_0,f_1,\dots,f_{K-1}$ of fixed predictors let $C_j:=\mathbf 1[f_j{=}Y]$,
$q_j(y):=P(C_j{=}1\mid Y{=}y)$, $a_j:=P(C_j{=}1)$, $b_j:=2a_j-1$,
$\delta_j:=2\big(q_j(1)-q_j(0)\big)$, and $c_{ij}:=2P(f_i{=}f_j)-1$ (observable). Recall
(\S\ref{sec:bsfrontier}) $\sign\Delta_a=\sign(b_a-b_0)$ on $D$.

The multi-candidate route (Proposition~\ref{thm:multicand}) used the identity
$c_{ij}=b_ib_j$ under conditional error-independence (CEI) alone. That is insufficient,
and the failure is exactly quantifiable:

\begin{theorem}[Exact rank-one deficit; the minimal hypothesis]\label{thm:H-deficit}
Under CEI (the $C_j$ mutually independent given $Y$ on $D$), for every $i\neq j$,
\[
  \boxed{\;c_{ij}-b_ib_j \;=\; \pi(1-\pi)\,\delta_i\,\delta_j\;}
\]
so $c=bb^\top+\pi(1-\pi)\,\delta\delta^\top$ off-diagonal: rank $\le 2$, not rank one.
For a panel with $K\ge3$ and all $b_j\neq0$ the rank-one law $c_{ij}=b_ib_j$ holds for
all pairs iff at most one $\delta_j\neq0$; uniformly in the panel iff $\delta_j=0$ for
all $j$. We call
\[
  \textbf{H}:\quad \text{CEI} \;+\; \text{per-class symmetric accuracy } q_j(0)=q_j(1)\ \forall j
\]
the minimal hypothesis, and state every result below under H.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$f_i{=}f_j\iff C_i{=}C_j$ in the binary setting, so
$c_{ij}=\E[(2C_i-1)(2C_j-1)]=(1-\pi)d_i(0)d_j(0)+\pi\,d_i(1)d_j(1)$ with $d_j(y):=2q_j(y)-1$,
using CEI to factorize given $Y$. Parametrize $d_i(0)=b_i-\pi\delta_i$,
$d_i(1)=b_i+(1-\pi)\delta_i$ (consistent with $(1-\pi)d_i(0)+\pi d_i(1)=b_i$); expanding,
the cross terms cancel and the $\delta_i\delta_j$ coefficient is
$(1-\pi)\pi^2+\pi(1-\pi)^2=\pi(1-\pi)$. The iff claims follow since $\pi(1-\pi)>0$ and
$\delta_i\delta_j=0$ for all pairs forces all but one $\delta_j$ to vanish when $K\ge3$.
\end{proof}

\noindent\emph{Validated} (\texttt{validation\_results.json:H\_hypothesis\_check}): under H the
off-diagonal error is Monte-Carlo noise ($5.5\times10^{-4}$ at $\pi{=}0.5$;
$4.8\times10^{-4}$ at $\pi{=}0.25$ --- the identity does \emph{not} require class
balance); under plain CEI with asymmetric accuracies the identity fails by $0.052$ while
the deficit matches $\pi(1-\pi)\delta_i\delta_j$ to $5.7\times10^{-4}$, and a $2\times2$
minor of $c$ is $-0.025\neq0$ (rank one fails). \emph{This corrects the hypothesis of
Proposition~\ref{thm:multicand} and is load-bearing for everything below; it also makes
precise the ``per-class symmetric-accuracy'' gap a referee identified.} Under H the
deficit vanishes identically, and departures from H re-enter through the residual
$\tau$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:no-verify}(b)).

\subsection{The one-bit theorem}\label{sec:onebit-thm}

\begin{theorem}[Identification up to one global bit]\label{thm:onebit-id}
Assume H, $K\ge3$, all $b_j\neq0$, and $\min_{i\ne j}|c_{ij}|\ge c_{\min}>0$. The full
label-free evidence law---the joint law of the prediction vector
$(f_0,\dots,f_{K-1})$ on $D$---determines (i) every magnitude $|b_j|$ via
$b_i^2=c_{ik}c_{il}/c_{kl}$, (ii) every product $b_ib_j=c_{ij}$ and hence every relative
sign $\sign(b_ib_j)$, and (iii) every benefit magnitude $|b_j-b_0|$. All are determined
exactly up to the global flip $b\mapsto-b$; the decision direction $\sign(b_j-b_0)$ is
not determined.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The flip witness: the bit is free at every moment order]\label{thm:flip-witness}
For any H-target $P$, let $P'$ complement the label ($Y'=1-Y$) leaving $P^X$ and all
predictors unchanged. Then $P'$ satisfies H; the full evidence law is \emph{identical},
$\mathrm{TV}\big(\mathrm{Law}(f_{0:K-1}\mid P),\mathrm{Law}(f_{0:K-1}\mid P')\big)=0$;
and $b'=-b$, so $\sign(b'_j-b'_0)=-\sign(b_j-b_0)$ for every candidate. Consequently no
label-free statistic of the prediction channel---of \emph{any} order, not merely
pairwise---can recover the bit, even inside the fully audited class H.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof of both]
Identification: ratios and products of the observable $c$ as displayed; well-defined by
$|c_{kl}|\ge c_{\min}$; $|b_j-b_0|^2=b_j^2-2c_{j0}+b_0^2$ is a function of $c$.
Flip-invariance of $c=bb^\top$ and of all magnitudes is immediate; $\sign(b_j-b_0)$
flips. Witness: predictions are maps of $X$ whose law is unchanged, so the evidence law
is identical (TV $=0$); $C'_j=1-C_j$ gives $b'_j=-b_j$; H is preserved since
$q'_j(y)=1-q_j(1-y)$ and per-class symmetry carries over; CEI is a relabeling.
\end{proof}

\noindent\emph{Remark (why third moments do not rescue the sign).} Spectral and tensor
methods (\textbf{Jaffe et al.} 2015; the Dawid--Skene line) are sometimes read as fixing
the sign via third-order statistics. Theorem~\ref{thm:flip-witness} shows what they can
and cannot do: observable moments of every order are flip-invariant, so third-order
structure identifies the latent class-conditional geometry only \emph{up to the label
permutation}---and for binary $Y$ that permutation \emph{is} the bit. The
better-than-chance anchors those works adopt are not estimators of the bit; they are
selections of it.

\begin{theorem}[Necessity: every identifying assumption is a bit-selector]\label{thm:bit-necessity}
Let $\mathcal C$ be any class of H-targets on which $\sign(b_a-b_0)$ is identifiable
from the evidence law. Then $\mathcal C$ contains at most one member of each flip-pair
$\{P,P'\}$ with $b_a\neq b_0$. Conversely, selecting one member of each flip-pair
suffices. Identifiability of the decision direction is therefore \emph{exactly} a
one-bit selection.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If both $P,P'\in\mathcal C$, Theorem~\ref{thm:flip-witness} puts opposite decision signs
on one evidence law, contradicting identifiability; sufficiency holds because on each
evidence-fiber the surviving member determines the sign.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The dictionary: published devices select the same bit]\label{thm:bit-dictionary}
Under H: (i) the $\gamma$-budget certificate with $|M|>\beta$ selects the bit
$\sign(M)$ (the flip sends $\gamma\to-\gamma$, fixes the observable $M$); (ii)
majority-above-chance (Proposition~\ref{thm:multicand}; \textbf{Parisi et al.} 2014)
selects $\sign(\mathrm{median}_j\,b_j)$; (iii) the anchored agreement-law slope
(Theorem~\ref{thm:agl}) selects $\sign(1-2\bar w)$, since the flip sends $w\mapsto1-w$
pointwise and hence negates the slope. Each device is sound iff its selected bit equals
the true bit; none is testable from the evidence channel
(Theorem~\ref{thm:flip-witness}).
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
(i) $\sign\Delta=\sign(M+\gamma)=\sign M$ when $|M|>\beta\ge|\gamma|$;
$\gamma=\E[\eta_a-s]\mapsto\E[(1-\eta_a)-s]=-\gamma-2(\E[s]-\tfrac12)+\dots$ --- computed
cleanly via $\gamma=b_a/2-M$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:gamma-id}): the flip negates $b_a$ and fixes
$M$. (ii) $b'_j=-b_j$ reverses every $\sign b_j$, hence the majority count. (iii) on
$D_\theta$ exactly one of $f_0,f_\theta$ is correct, so $w'(\theta)=1-w(\theta)$ and
$1-2\bar w\mapsto-(1-2\bar w)$.
\end{proof}

\noindent\emph{Validated} (\texttt{V1\_flip\_witness}; Fig.~\ref{fig:onebit}):
$\max_j|b'_j+b_j|=1.1\times10^{-16}$, evidence TV $=0$ exactly, agreement products
invariant to MC precision, all benefit signs opposite.

