\noindent\emph{Validated} (\texttt{V3\_audit}; Fig.~\ref{fig:audit}): level
$0.000\le\alpha{=}0.05$ over 600 trials at $m{=}6000$; power $1.000$ at
$\min_{\mathrm{flip}}|\gamma|=0.216>\beta{=}0.05$ (bootstrap radius). \emph{Honest
caveat, proven and reported:} the worst-case constant $C_2$ is large at small $c_{\min}$
($C_2\approx84$ at $c_{\min}{=}0.18$), making the worst-case radius sound but nearly
powerless at realistic $m$ (rejection $0.000$ in the same sweep); the deployable test
uses a bootstrap radius and the worst-case version supplies the guarantee. Closing this
gap (a tight finite-sample radius) is stated as an open problem below.

\begin{theorem}[Verification impossibility; the audit's exact blind set]\label{thm:no-verify}
\textnormal{(a) Within H.} No label-free test can accept ``$|\gamma|\le\beta$'' with
power: verification fails exactly on the bit-ambiguous set
\[
  \mathcal B_\beta=\Big\{\;\big|\,|M|-\tfrac{|b_a|}{2}\big|\;\le\;\beta\;<\;|M|+\tfrac{|b_a|}{2}\;\Big\},
\]
on which one bit satisfies the budget, the other violates it, and both share one
evidence law (Theorem~\ref{thm:flip-witness}); a sound procedure must abstain there, and
the audit of Theorem~\ref{thm:audit} correctly does not reject.
\textnormal{(b) Outside H.} The overdetermination residual $\tau$
(Proposition~\ref{prop:cei-test}) is \emph{sound but not complete}: there exist
strictly interior $K{=}4$ correctness laws with every pairwise $c_{ij}$ exactly rank-one
($\tau=0$) that violate CEI and leave $b$ unidentified---including witnesses whose
fitted decision sign is wrong. What $\tau=0$ pins down is precisely that $c$ is
rank-one, i.e.\ \emph{pairwise-realizability by some H-model}; higher-order moments
remain free.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(a) is Lemma~\ref{lem:gamma-id} plus Theorem~\ref{thm:flip-witness}: the two flip values
of $\gamma$ straddle the budget exactly on $\mathcal B_\beta$. (b) is a linear-program
construction on the $2^4$ correctness-pattern probabilities: impose the six pairwise
second-moment equations $\E[S_iS_j]=b^{\mathrm{fit}}_ib^{\mathrm{fit}}_j$, total mass,
nonnegativity, and a first-moment constraint $\E[S_1]\neq b^{\mathrm{fit}}_1$; the
Chebyshev center is strictly interior, realizes $\tau=0$, and its third moment
$\E[S_1S_2S_3]\neq\prod_i\E[S_i]$ certifies the CEI violation. Full details and the
cached witnesses: \texttt{theory\_v2/THEORY\_V2\_PROOFS.md},
\texttt{stealth\_law.json}, \texttt{stealth\_flip.json}.
\end{proof}

\noindent\emph{Validated} (\texttt{V3\_audit.stealth\_tau0},
\texttt{V3\_audit.bit\_ambiguous\_blind}): design $\tau=2.1\times10^{-17}$ with recovery
bias $0.229$; a second witness flips the recovered decision sign ($+1$ vs.\ true $-1$)
at $\tau\approx0$; on $\mathcal B_\beta$ the audit rejects at rate $0.000$ (correctly
blind). Against \emph{overt} dependence the residual is informative: $\tau$ rises from
$2\times10^{-4}$ to $0.119$ as error-correlation $\rho$ goes $0\to0.6$ (Pearson
$0.914$).

\smallskip\noindent\emph{Honest scope.} Falsify-not-verify is the classical epistemic
position of exchangeability testing (\textbf{Vovk et al.}) and assumption-alarms appear
in the algebraic-evaluation program (\textbf{Corrada-Emmanuel}, NeurIPS 2024) and in
dependent-classifier diagnostics (\textbf{Jaffe et al.} 2016); \textbf{Rosenfeld \&
Garg} (2023) bound error under an explicitly unverifiable assumption. The new content
here is the \emph{statistical} level-$\alpha$ rejection test for a \emph{continuous
budget} on a quantity that is unobservable yet point-identified up to a provably
irreducible bit; the exact blind set $\mathcal B_\beta$; and the constructive
incompleteness of $\tau$. Within this framework the earlier budget devices
(Propositions on $\eta$-transfer, $\beta$-drift, $B$-drift) stop being unexaminable:
each budget premise acquires a sound falsifier, and what no falsifier can ever reach is
characterized exactly ($\mathcal B_\beta$ within H; rank-one-preserving violations
outside H, Theorem~\ref{thm:no-verify}(b)).

\subsection{The evidence-channel rate}\label{sec:evrate}

\begin{theorem}[Matching $m^{-1/2}$ bounds for the label-free channel]\label{thm:ev-rate}
Within audited H with margins ($c_{\min}$, $b_{\min}$) and given the bit:
\textnormal{(a)} the product-ratio estimator satisfies, with probability $\ge1-\delta$,
$|\hat b_j-b_j|\le C(c_{\min},b_{\min})\sqrt{\log(K/\delta)/m}$ with
$C=\Theta\big(1/(b_{\min}c_{\min}^2)\big)$, so $\sign(b_j-b_0)$ is certified at sample
complexity $m\asymp C^2(b_j-b_0)^{-2}\log(K/\delta)$;
\textnormal{(b)} a Le Cam two-point argument inside H (two models differing by
$\varepsilon$ in one coordinate; the per-sample KL between prediction-pattern laws is
$\le C'\varepsilon^2$) shows no estimator can beat $m^{-1/2}$;
\textnormal{(c)} consequently labels buy \emph{constants and the bit, not the rate}: the
labeled-vs-evidence efficiency ratio is constant in $m$ but scales like $1/c_{\min}$ and
diverges as pairwise agreement approaches chance.
\end{theorem}

