from \emph{labeled} paired benefits, whereas Theorem~\ref{thm:ev-rate} is the genuinely
label-free rate. The constant's $1/c_{\min}^2$ dependence is real and reported, not
optimized; tightening it is open.

\subsection{Consolidation and what remains open}\label{sec:consolidation}

\begin{corollary}[The budget propositions, unified]\label{cor:unify-budgets}
Under H, the calibration-transfer premise (Prop.~9), the $\beta$-drift premise
(Prop.~11), the bounded-drift premise (Prop.~13), and the majority anchor
(Prop.~\ref{thm:multicand}) each decompose as
\emph{(testable structure)} $+$ \emph{(one bit)}: the structure is falsifiable by the
audit of Theorem~\ref{thm:audit} together with the residual $\tau$, and the bit is
irreducible by Theorem~\ref{thm:bit-necessity}. In this precise sense the paper's
trichotomy is complete: \textsc{adapt}/\textsc{freeze} when the audited structure plus a
declared bit certify the sign with margin; \textsc{abstain} when the audit rejects, or
when the margin falls inside the blind set $\mathcal B_\beta$.
\end{corollary}

\noindent\textbf{Open problems (the honest frontier).} (1) A tight (non-conservative)
finite-sample radius for the audit---the proven $C_2$ constant is loose; the bootstrap
version is powerful but its validity is empirical. (2) The weakest falsifiable
structural class strictly containing H for which the one-bit reduction survives
(Conjecture~\ref{conj:gen}, now sharpened: any such class must exclude the
rank-one-preserving stealth laws of Theorem~\ref{thm:no-verify}(b)). (3) Per-class
asymmetric panels: quantitative recovery when $\delta\neq0$ but $\|\delta\|$ is small,
via the exact deficit of Theorem~\ref{thm:H-deficit}.
